dc bridge: destroy handles when the client destroys or releases a DC object

The DC bridge was carried over from DDK 1.11, where PVRSRVReleaseHandleUnlocked() destroyed a
handle. In 1.19 it only drops a lookup reference; the call that destroys a handle is
PVRSRVDestroyHandleStagedUnlocked(), as the other 1.19 bridges use. So DCDeviceRelease,
DCSystemBufferRelease, DCDisplayContextDestroy, DCBufferFree, DCBufferUnimport, DCBufferUnpin
and DCBufferRelease destroyed nothing and returned whatever eError an earlier call had left in
the pooled reply buffer. Every display context (with its "pvr_misr" workqueue thread), buffer
and pin then lived as long as the connection. The reply now carries the real result, so a
RETRY (e.g. a display context that still has flips queued) keeps the handle and goes back to
the client to try again. Found on the TrimUI Brick, 2026-10-18.

--- a/generated/rogue/dc_bridge/server_dc_bridge.c
+++ b/generated/rogue/dc_bridge/server_dc_bridge.c
@@ -258,9 +258,16 @@
 	/* Lock over handle destruction. */
 	LockHandle(psConnection->psHandleBase);
 
-	PVRSRVReleaseHandleUnlocked(psConnection->psHandleBase,
-				    (IMG_HANDLE) psDCDeviceReleaseIN->
-				    hDevice, PVRSRV_HANDLE_TYPE_DC_DEVICE);
+	psDCDeviceReleaseOUT->eError =
+	    PVRSRVDestroyHandleStagedUnlocked(psConnection->psHandleBase,
+					      (IMG_HANDLE) psDCDeviceReleaseIN->hDevice,
+					      PVRSRV_HANDLE_TYPE_DC_DEVICE);
+	if (unlikely((psDCDeviceReleaseOUT->eError != PVRSRV_OK) &&
+		     !PVRSRVIsRetryError(psDCDeviceReleaseOUT->eError)))
+	{
+		PVR_DPF((PVR_DBG_ERROR, "%s: %s", __func__,
+			 PVRSRVGetErrorString(psDCDeviceReleaseOUT->eError)));
+	}
 
 	/* Release now we have destroyed handles. */
 	UnlockHandle(psConnection->psHandleBase);
